# AccessBuddy As-Built Architecture Review

- Reviewer: Distinguished Systems Architect (read-only, no production mutation)
- Repo: `/home/nicholas-wood/Documents/workspace/projects/accessbuddy/code` @ HEAD `7becae0`
- Product state: live v1.10.2, one tenant (SMLSC, 192 members), about to onboard tenant #2 and introduce SaaS pricing
- Date: 2026-07-02

## Assessment

AccessBuddy is a single-service, multi-tenant Next.js 15 application on GCP Cloud Run (`australia-southeast1`), backed by one Cloud SQL Postgres 16 instance (`db-f1-micro`), with Firebase Auth, Stripe Connect Express, Resend and Sentry. Tenancy is a shared schema keyed by a `tenant_id` FK on every tenant table, intended to be enforced at two layers: Postgres row level security (RLS) and a membership-scoped application authorisation wrapper. For a solo founder this is a disciplined build: infrastructure is almost entirely in Terraform, deploy auth uses Workload Identity Federation with no long-lived keys, most secrets are in Secret Manager, and the decision history is unusually well documented.

The review changes one headline claim the codebase makes about itself. The RLS layer is well designed but, on the evidence, inert in production: the application connects with a database role that bypasses RLS, so tenant isolation currently rests entirely on the application layer. That is the pivotal finding for the 1-to-N jump and is detailed as F1.

